# WI-15 — label-state probes

What this file is: the recorded, read-only probes that establish the shape of the issue-label
state the harness reads, so the `readIssueLabels` parse is written against **observed** gh output
rather than an assumption. Every command below is read-only. Dates: 2026-09-23 (probes 1–3, run
during T2). Later probes are appended by the task that runs them, each with its own command, exit
code and verbatim output.

The confounder this file exists to remove: the issue this work item is about was caused by the
harness interpreting a subprocess's *text* instead of reading its *state* — so the shape of the
state is probed first, before any parse is written against it.

---

## Probe 1 — the plan's owner-approved shape probe (fixture repo)

Command:

```
gh issue list --repo manjula25/loop-fixtures-py --state all --limit 5 --json number,labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim stdout:

```
[{"labels":[],"number":54},{"labels":[],"number":52},{"labels":[],"number":49},{"labels":[],"number":48},{"labels":[],"number":45}]
```

What it establishes: gh's `--json labels` field puts the labels under a `labels` **array key** per
issue (so `JSON.parse(stdout)` yields an array of issues, each with a `labels` array). What it
does **not** establish: the shape of an array *element* — every array here is empty, so this probe
alone cannot distinguish `{name: string}` objects from bare strings. It was recorded as
inconclusive on that point rather than read as if it had settled it; probes 2 and 3 below supply
the missing element shape. **This is the fork the plan flagged, reached honestly.**

## Probe 2 — is any issue in the fixture repo even labelled?

Command:

```
gh issue list --repo manjula25/loop-fixtures-py --state all --limit 100 --json number,labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim result: 24 issues returned, and the filtered list of issues carrying at least one label
was empty:

```
[]
total issues: 24
```

What it establishes: no issue in the synthetic fixture repo wears a label, so the element shape
cannot be observed there at any `--limit`. Probe 1's emptiness is a property of the fixture data,
not of gh's serialisation — which is why the element shape had to be observed elsewhere.

## Probe 3 — the element shape, on a public repo with a labelled issue

Command (same `--json labels` field as probe 1, on a public repository):

```
gh issue list --repo cli/cli --state all --limit 3 --json number,labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim stdout:

```
[{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}],"number":14512},{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}],"number":14511},{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}],"number":14510}]
```

What it establishes: a `labels` array element is an **object carrying `name`** (alongside `id`,
`description`, `color`) — not a bare string. The plan's expected case holds, so the parse is
`.labels.map((l) => l.name)`; `.map(String)` would have been wrong here (it would yield
`"[object Object]"` and every membership check would fail).

## Probe 3b — the wiring's own subcommand, on a labelled issue

The wiring calls `gh issue view <n> --json labels` (not `gh issue list`), so the element shape is
confirmed on that subcommand too. The probe adds `--repo cli/cli` only to point that same
subcommand at a repository whose issue carries a label; the wiring itself omits `--repo` and is
scoped by the target repo's cwd instead, so this is the wiring's subcommand and `--json` field,
not its literal argv.

```
gh issue view 14512 --repo cli/cli --json labels
```

Exit code: `0`

Verbatim stdout:

```
{"labels":[{"id":"LA_kwDODKw3uc7QD3p7","name":"needs-triage","description":"needs to be reviewed","color":"D6393F"}]}
```

What it establishes: `issue view --json labels` returns the same top-level `{"labels":[...]}`
object with the same element shape, so `JSON.parse(raw) as { labels: readonly { name: string }[] }`
followed by `.map((l) => l.name)` matches observed output. (Consistent with the shape already
recorded in `docs/work/WI-15/prd.md`: `gh issue view 54 --json labels,state` →
`{"labels":[],"state":"CLOSED"}`, exit 0 — that record confirms the top-level key, this probe
confirms the element.)